/K [6]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (A plain and textual reading of Section 20 reveals that the legislature has consciously confined the rule-making authority to prescribe the manner of distribution of credit by an Input Service Distributor. The provision neither expressly nor impliedly authorises prescription of a time-limit. In fiscal jurisprudence, when Parliament intends to impose a limitation period affecting substantive rights, it does so in clear terms. The conspicuous silence of Section 20 on any temporal restriction is therefore legally meaningful. The rule-making authority cannot, under the guise of regulating procedure, introduce a substantive condition that extinguishes or forfeits a vested statutory entitlement.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 95 0 R
/E (The principle is well-settled. In Sales Tax Officer v. K. I. Abraham \(1967\) 20 STC 367, the Supreme Court held that a rule-making authority cannot introduce a period of limitation when the statute itself does not prescribe one. Likewise, in Lakshmi Rattan Engineering Works Ltd. v. CST AIR 1968 SC 488, it was observed that procedural rules are meant to advance justice and not defeat substantive rights. The impugned rule, by imposing an absolute monthly bar, had the effect of denying legitimately accrued credit, thereby defeating the object of Section 20.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
