/P 98 0 R
/E (Despite this, the Revisional Authority exercised powers to reverse the appellate order, proceeding on the premise that unloading at an unregistered place was itself sufficient to attract penal consequences. The High Court found this approach unsustainable. It observed that the appellate order was a well-reasoned one, passed after appreciation of evidence, and that the Revisional Authority had failed to demonstrate how the appellate decision was either erroneous in law or prejudicial to the interests of revenue. The Court emphasised that revisional powers are not appellate powers and cannot be invoked merely because the revisional authority holds a different or stricter view on the same set of facts. Mere technical non-compliance, without any element of fraud, suppression or intention to evade tax, does not confer jurisdiction for revision. On these findings, the revisional order was quashed, the appellate order was restored, and the writ petition was allowed.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
