>>
endobj
400 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (This decision reinforces the now-settled doctrinal position that liquidated damages are compensatory in nature and do not constitute consideration for a taxable supply under GST. From a contractual perspective, liquidated damages arise solely upon breach or non-performance and operate as a pre-estimated measure of loss agreed between the parties. Sections 73 and 74 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872 statutorily characterise such payments as compensation for injury suffered due to breach, and not as consideration flowing from any consensual or reciprocal arrangement.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
401 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
402 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Significantly, Circular No. 178/10/2022-GST, which itself acknowledges that amounts which are purely compensatory and arise from breach of contract, without any element of agreeing to tolerate such breach, fall outside the scope of GST. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [9 10]
>>
endobj
