/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (The Court further examined the manner in which the demand was sustained by relying upon GST back-office data and internal system-generated information, which had not been disclosed to the assessee. It was observed that data residing in the GST back-office is within the exclusive knowledge and control of the tax authorities, and denial of access to such material deprives the taxpayer of an effective opportunity to rebut the allegations. The High Court held that reliance on undisclosed material violates principles of natural justice and renders the adjudication unsustainable. Consequently, the appellate order was set aside and the matter was remitted for fresh adjudication, with a direction that the proceedings must remain confined to the issues in the original show-cause notice and that all back-office data relied upon must be disclosed to the assessee. The Court also clarified that objections relating to limitation would survive only if they were available at the time of issuance of the original notice.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
