endobj
458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (Section 75\(7\) is not a mere procedural formality; it is a substantive limitation on adjudicatory power. The provision mandates that tax, interest and penalty must be determined strictly in accordance with the grounds specified in the show-cause notice. This statutory discipline was consciously introduced to remedy the mischief prevalent under earlier indirect tax regimes, where adjudicating authorities routinely travelled beyond the notice and confirmed demands on entirely new grounds. The Supreme Court in Commissioner of Customs, Mumbai v. Toyo Engineering India Limited\(2006 AIR SCW 5167\) unequivocally held that the department cannot go beyond the scope of the show-cause notice. Section 75\(7\) now codifies this principle within the GST framework.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (In the present case, the authorities effectively altered the foundation of the demand by recharacterising a substantive issue relating to chargeability as a mere matter of quantification. Such recasting of the cause of action is impermissible. The show-cause notice defines the contours of the lis; it is the jurisdictional foundation of adjudication. Any attempt to travel beyond it amounts to assumption of authority not conferred by statute.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (That said, the decision to remand the matter for fresh adjudication invites closer scrutiny. Once the original show-cause notice is found defective for travelling beyond its scope, the question arises whether remand effectively permits the revenue to cure a jurisdictional defect retrospectively. Section 75\(7\) is intended as a protective shield; allowing expansion of the demand through remand risks diluting its legislative purpose. If the lis was confined to a particular ground, there is no statutory basis for permitting introduction of a new cause of action under the guise of reconsideration.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
461 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 106 0 R
/E (An additional jurisdictional aspect also merits attention. The allegation recorded was one of suppression of turnover. Suppression, by its very nature, attracts the extended limitation and penal consequences under Section 74. Invocation of Section 73 in such circumstances presents an internal inconsistency in the show-cause notice itself. A notice alleging suppression yet issued under Section 73 suffer from foundational infirmity.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
